Q: Map set to a value if it is a subset of a key set A: <blockquote>
<p>I was wondering if there is a way to simplify this, or is the if-elif chain the best?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The <code>if-elif</code> wins on clarity.  I would stick with it unless there are many cases.</p>
<p>When there are a lot of cases, you can apply binary search to reduce the number of tests.  That will require pre-computing combinations of the test groups.  Only do this if speed is important.</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code># test groups
pool1 = {'a', 'b', 'c'}
pool2 = {'d', 'e', 'f'}
pool3 = {'g', 'h', 'i'}
pool4 = {'j', 'k', 'l'}

# precomputed combinations
pool_1_2 = pool1 | pool2;  assert pool1.isdisjoint(pool2)
pool_3_4 = pool3 | pool4;  assert pool3.isdisjoint(pool4)

def classify(valueset):
    if valueset &lt;= pool_1_2:
        if valueset &lt;= pool1:
            return 'one'
        elif valueset &lt;= pool2:
            return 'two'
    elif valueset &lt;= pool_3_4:
        if valueset &lt;= pool3:
            return 'three'
        elif valueset &lt;= pool4:
            return 'four'
    else:
        return 'no match'

# Nested tests
valueset = {'g', 'h'}
print(classify(valueset))  
</code></pre>

Q: How to test if expression contains specific value with NSubstitute A: <p>Well, for complex object creation with fake data you can use <a href="https://autofixture.github.io" rel="nofollow noreferrer">AutoFixture</a> nuget library.</p>
<p>Something like below:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>
[Test]
public void Test()
{
    var senderId = &quot;senderId&quot;;
    var expectedValue = &quot;expectedValue&quot;;

    var fakeRepo = Substitute.For&lt;IRepo&gt;();
    fakeRepo.TestMethod(
             Arg.Is((Expression&lt;Func&lt;Sender, bool&gt;&gt; expr) =&gt; expr.Compile()(
                 new Fixture()
                    .Build&lt;Sender&gt;()
                    .With(x =&gt; x.SenderId, senderId)
                    .Create()))
        ).Returns(expectedValue);

    var result = fakeRepo.TestMethod(x =&gt; x.SenderId == senderId);
    var result2 = fakeRepo.TestMethod(x =&gt; x.SenderId == &quot;randomId&quot;);

    Assert.That(result, Is.EqualTo(expectedValue));
    Assert.That(result2, Is.Empty);
}
</code></pre>
